<Base title="Myths — Catalog of Complexity" description="Complexity myths this catalog does not repeat.">
  <h1 class="page-title">Complexity myths this catalog does not repeat</h1>
  <p class="page-lede">
    Complexity science has a folklore layer — claims repeated so often in survey talks and
    introductory chapters that they read as settled fact. Several do not survive contact with
    the primary literature. The catalog's schema was built to name each one explicitly and bind
    entries to what the evidence actually supports, not to the folklore.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>&ldquo;Everything is a power law / scale-free&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      Power-law claims are the single most over-generalized empirical pattern in complexity
      science. Clauset, Shalizi &amp; Newman's statistical audit ("Power-law distributions in
      empirical data," <em>SIAM Review</em> 51(4), 2009) re-fit dozens of famous power-law claims
      with a rigorous maximum-likelihood-plus-goodness-of-fit procedure, rather than the
      eyeballed log&ndash;log regression that produced many of the original claims, and found that
      most of them do not survive: some distributions once called power laws are better
      described as log-normal or truncated alternatives, and even cases with a genuine power-law
      tail often cover a narrower range than advertised. The failures are not exotic edge cases —
      they include some of the field's most frequently cited examples. Closer to home for this
      catalog, Newman's own network survey ("The structure and function of complex networks,"
      <em>SIAM Review</em> 45(2), 2003) shows that not every network is scale-free either: the
      Western US power grid's degree distribution is exponential, not a power law.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the catalog handles it:</strong> the <code>power-law-rigor</code> filling rule.
      A power-law claim enters the catalog only with CSN-2009-grade statistical support (a fitted
      exponent plus a goodness-of-fit test against alternatives), or it is explicitly labeled
      <code>evidence_status: measured-untested</code>. Degree distributions are recorded per
      entry rather than assumed; the power grid entry records an exponential distribution, not a
      forced power-law fit.
    </p>

    <h2>&ldquo;Earthquakes are the textbook self-organized-criticality example&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      The Gutenberg&ndash;Richter law — earthquake frequency falling off as a power of magnitude —
      is genuinely solid statistics, replicated across seismic networks for decades. What is
      contested is the further step of calling this <em>self-organized criticality</em> as a
      mechanism: that the crust tunes itself to a critical state through its own dynamics, the
      way a sandpile does in the original SOC models. Watkins, Pruessner, Chapman, Crosby &amp;
      Jensen's review ("25 Years of Self-organized Criticality: Concepts and Controversies,"
      <em>Space Science Reviews</em> 198, 2016) is an explicitly adjudicating, not promotional,
      survey of the field a quarter-century on, and it treats the SOC-as-mechanism claim for
      earthquakes — and for most systems outside the original sandpile/rice-pile physics
      experiments — as unresolved rather than established.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the catalog handles it:</strong> the <code>soc-two-claims</code> filling rule.
      "Power-law event statistics" and "self-organized criticality as mechanism" are recorded as
      separate claims, never conflated. An entry can score solidly on the event-statistics side
      while its SOC-mechanism status stays marked contested — no catalog entry currently reaches
      the top of the criticality scale, by design, because none has an uncontested SOC mechanism
      behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>&ldquo;Markets exhibit low-dimensional chaos&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      Early nonlinear-dynamics work on stock returns (Scheinkman &amp; LeBaron, 1989) reported
      fractal-dimension estimates in weekly returns that were popularly read as evidence of
      deterministic chaos with only a handful of driving variables. That reading has been
      substantially walked back. The subsequent BDS-test and denoising literature — statistical
      tests designed specifically to distinguish nonlinear stochastic dependence from genuine
      low-dimensional chaos — finds nonlinear <em>dependence</em> (volatility clustering,
      GARCH-type structure) in large return series but not low-dimensional chaos; noise cannot be
      reliably separated from chaos with the tests available, and the field's settled position is
      "nonlinearity yes, low-dimensional chaos no."
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the catalog handles it:</strong> the <code>no-market-chaos</code> filling rule.
      Financial-markets entries record "nonlinear dependence: yes, deterministic chaos: no
      evidence" rather than a chaos verdict, and cite the historical claim alongside its
      correction rather than repeating the claim alone.
    </p>

    <h2>&ldquo;Climate maximizes entropy production&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      The Maximum Entropy Production (MEP) hypothesis — that the climate system organizes itself
      toward a state that maximizes the rate of thermodynamic entropy production — is a real,
      live research program, not a fabrication. Paltridge's original proposal (1975, 1978) showed
      that simplified radiative-convective models tuned this way reproduce plausible
      latitudinal temperature and cloud patterns. But per the standard review (Ozawa, Ohmura,
      Lorenz &amp; Pujol, "The second law of thermodynamics and the global climate system: A
      review of the maximum entropy production principle," <em>Reviews of Geophysics</em> 41(4),
      2003), MEP has no accepted first-principles derivation from the equations of motion, and the
      climate-science community remains skeptical that it is a useful organizing principle beyond
      a curve-fitting device. It is a contested hypothesis, not settled physics — and not a
      quantity the catalog can tabulate as a measured "entropy production rate of Earth's
      climate."
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the catalog handles it:</strong> recorded per entry with
      <code>evidence_status: measured-disputed</code>, distinct from ordinary "unknown." The
      climate entry's entropy-production discussion is treated as rubric-scored narrative around
      a contested theory, not as a filled quantitative column.
    </p>

    <h2>The CSN-vs-Gutenberg-Richter tension</h2>
    <p>
      This one is not folklore to debunk but a genuine tension the catalog records rather than
      silently resolving. The classic seismological Gutenberg&ndash;Richter b-value for California
      earthquakes (b&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;1) and Clauset, Shalizi &amp; Newman's own rigorous power-law
      fit to California earthquake data (&alpha;&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;1.64) look like they should be the
      same statistical object measured two ways — but CSN's stricter test actually
      <em>rejects</em> the power-law hypothesis for that data (p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.00), while the
      traditional Gutenberg&ndash;Richter fit is widely treated as solid. The two analyses differ
      in variable (log-amplitude magnitude vs. raw amplitude) and in fitting rigor, so the results
      do not simply convert into one another. Presenting either number alone, without the other,
      would misrepresent how settled earthquake statistics actually are.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How the catalog handles it:</strong> both figures are recorded, attributed to their
      respective methodologies, with the tension stated explicitly in the entry rather than
      collapsed into a single "the b-value is 1" claim.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="block prose">
    <h2>Why this list stays honest</h2>
    <p>
      This register is enforced by machine-checkable rules and adversarial verifiers, not
      editorial goodwill. Every entry passes through a citation checker, a plausibility checker,
      a consistency checker, and a coverage checker before promotion, and the filling rules named
      above are binding on both the research agents that draft entries and the verifiers that
      check them. See the <a href="/methods/">methods</a> page for how that pipeline works.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
